  "medium": {
    "heading": "Finding the Rhythm in the Medium Grid",
    "body": "The medium puzzle today definitely made me pause and think for a second, but it was so rewarding once I found the hook. The big breakthrough for me was that sum region on the top right. It covers three cells at 1,4, 1,5, and 2,5, and they all have to add up to just 1. That is a huge hint! It means two of those spots have to be zero and one has to be a one. I looked at the dominoes for any that had zeros or ones, like the 4,0 or the 1,5, and started testing how they could sit next to each other.\n\nAnother tricky spot was the greater than 9 region in the middle. To get a sum higher than 9 using only two cells, you really need some of the big hitters. I used the 6,4 domino there because it adds up to exactly 10, which is just enough to pass the test. After that, the empty cells at 2,0 and 4,0 acted like little walls that helped me figure out where the 3,0 and 1,0 dominoes had to live. It felt a bit like a game of Tetris, just trying to see which shapes could actually squeeze into the open spaces left over.",
  },
  "hard": {
    "heading": "Cracking the Code on Today's Hardest Board",
    "body": "Wow, the hard puzzle today really put my brain to work! I spent a good five minutes just staring at it before I made my first move. The key for me was that 12 sum over at 2,0 and 3,0. Since the highest number we have is a 6, both of those cells absolutely had to be 6s. That meant the dominoes at 1,0 and 3,1 both had to be connected to a 6. I grabbed the 1,6 and 3,6 dominoes and placed them carefully, which gave me a great starting point on the left side of the board.\n\nThe real challenge was the massive equals region that snakes through the middle at cells like 2,2, 2,4, and 3,4. When you have that many cells that all need the same number of pips, it really limits your choices. I realized they all had to be 4s to make the surrounding sums work, especially the 10 sum at the top right. There was a moment where I thought I had stuck myself in a corner near the bottom where the 9,3 cell needs to sum to 6, but after swapping a few dominoes around, everything finally lined up. It was a tough one, but man, that feeling when the last domino fits is the best!",
